FILE SYSTEMS — Data Is Stored, Organized, and Managed

CORE IDEAS
Organize  — directory trees, metadata, allocation maps
Store     — blocks/clusters, journaling vs copy-on-write
Secure    — permissions, encryption, snapshots

FAT32
Used in: USB drives, SD cards
Fundamentals: Simple allocation table, high compatibility
Limits: Max file size 4 GB
Best for: Small removable media across almost any OS

exFAT
Used in: Flash drives, SDXC cards
Fundamentals: Extended FAT without the 4 GB file cap
Strengths: Large file support, cross-platform (Windows, macOS, Linux, cameras)
Best for: Large files on removable storage

NTFS
Used in: Windows system and data drives
Fundamentals: Journaling, ACLs, compression, encryption (EFS)
Strengths: Security & permissions, file encryption, large volumes
Best for: Windows internal drives

ext4
Used in: Linux
Fundamentals: Journaling, extents, delayed allocation
Strengths: Stability, performance, large partitions
Best for: Linux root and data partitions

APFS
Used in: macOS, iOS
Fundamentals: Copy-on-write, snapshots, space sharing, encryption
Strengths: Optimized for SSD, snapshots, encryption
Best for: Modern Apple devices

HFS+
Used in: Older Macs
Fundamentals: Journaled hierarchical file system
Strengths: Reliable & fast for its era
Note: Predecessor to APFS

NFS
Used in: Linux/Unix networks
Fundamentals: Network File System protocol (remote mount)
Strengths: Network file sharing, remote access
Best for: Unix-like server-client file access

SMB
Used in: Windows file sharing (also Samba on Linux)
Fundamentals: Server Message Block protocol
Strengths: Easy file sharing, authentication, access control
Best for: Cross-machine sharing on Windows networks

